## The invariant payload

An invariant is not necessarily a literal word. It can be any relation that must survive translation:

- identity or role;
- direction, order, or causal orientation;
- a difference or ratio between elements;
- ethical boundaries and permissions;
- topology: which things remain connected;
- transformation law: how paired elements change together;
- provenance: where an idea came from and how it changed;
- retrieval anchors needed to reconstruct a larger structure.

For an invariant set $I=\{I_1,\ldots,I_k\}$, define a semantic checksum:

\[
\chi_I(x)=\bigl(I_1(x),\ldots,I_k(x)\bigr)
\]

A mutation passes when its weighted checksum loss remains below tolerance:

\[
L_I(x,x')=
\sum_{j=1}^{k} w_j\,d_j\bigl(I_j(x),I_j(x')\bigr)
\le \varepsilon
\]

This generalizes the **Delta Twins** color insight: an absolute value may change while the relationship between paired values remains stable. If both colors move under the same transform, their relational delta can preserve the perceived structure even when the original color is missing. Auto Incorrect applies that same law to words, stories, roles, equations, code, music, therapy loops, and model prompts.

