## Erratum: an error that has acquired an address

An **error** is a deviation relative to a rule or expectation. An **erratum** is more useful: it is a deviation that has been located, acknowledged, and attached to a repair record. âErrataâ are therefore not merely a pile of mistakes. Historically and architecturally, they are an appended layer that lets the original artifact remain visible while a correction travels beside it.

The word also carries the older sense of wandering or straying. Auto Incorrect makes that wandering bounded and instrumented.

\[
\operatorname{Erratum}
=
(\text{deviation},\text{address},\text{repair},
\text{provenance},\text{retained delta})
\]

This yields a useful ladder:

| State | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **Noise** | Variation with no recoverable relation |
| **Error** | A rule or invariant was violated |
| **Erratum** | The violation has an address and repair |
| **Productive erratum** | The repair exposes a useful latent relation |
| **Kintsugi doctrine** | The useful delta is integrated while the seam remains visible |

The distinction matters because silent correction destroys evidence. Errata preserve the route by which a system became less wrong. In code they resemble patches and traces; in science, residuals and anomaly logs; in navigation, the error signal that makes steering possible; in biology, mutation plus repair; in learning, prediction error plus model update.

